+A contributor's `~/.claude/agents/` may additionally define specialised reviewers (`rust-pro`,
+`architect-reviewer`, `code-reviewer`, `security-auditor`). Those are an **optional upgrade** for the
+review trio, never a requirement: this skill must run unchanged in a fresh clone, so every mandatory
+step above resolves to a built-in.
+
+**One call, or several in one message.** Dispatch sequential, order-dependent steps (implementer
+tasks, spec/plan critique loops, spec-compliance review) as a **single `Agent` call** — the call
+returns the agent's report, and you need that result before deciding the next action. The mandatory
+review trio (Phase 4 step 8) is three **independent** read-only passes: issue all three `Agent`
+calls **in the same message** so they run concurrently rather than one after another. Concurrent
+agents run in the background; each one's result arrives as a task-completion notification, so you
+can keep working on something else (e.g. drafting the PR-comment aggregation skeleton) while they
+run. Collect a result from its completion notification, or continue a still-running agent with
+`SendMessage` addressed to it by name/ID. Never predict or invent a pending agent's findings — if a
+notification has not arrived, the pass has not reported yet.
+
+**Progress tracking** is `TodoWrite` (see Phase 0), not a per-dispatch field.
+
+**Model selection.** Where the plan/spec calls for "mechanical, 1–2-file task" pass
+`model: "haiku"`. For multi-file integration work or design-judgment tasks, omit `model` so the
+dispatch inherits strong reasoning — but note that an omitted `model` resolves to the agent
+definition's model first, then the configured default subagent model, and only then the parent
+session's, so omission **alone does not guarantee** strong reasoning. Where the session's default
+subagent model is known to be weaker than the orchestrator's, pass `model: "opus"` explicitly on
+design-judgment dispatches. There is no `reasoning_effort` parameter — model choice is the only
+effort dial.
 
